22:43:07 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Jet engines don’t directly lift a plane—they provide the forward thrust that makes lift possible. Here’s how the whole system works together to keep an aircraft in the air. ### 1. The jet engine’s job: thrust A jet engine moves the plane forward by pushing a large mass of air backward at high speed. In simple terms: - **Suck**: A fan at the front draws in air. - **Squeeze**: The air is compressed by spinning blades, raising its pressure and temperature. - **Bang**: Fuel is injected and burned in the combustion chamber, superheating the air and making it expand violently. - **Blow**: The hot, high-pressure gases blast out the rear nozzle. According to Newton’s third law, the backward rush of exhaust pushes the engine—and the whole plane—forward. This forward force is called **thrust**. ### 2. How forward motion creates lift Once the plane is moving fast enough, its wings begin to generate the upward force (lift) that counters weight. Two main effects are at work: - **Pressure difference (Bernoulli’s principle)**: A wing is shaped like an airfoil—curved on top and flatter on the bottom. As air flows around it, the air travelling over the curved upper surface must move faster to keep up with the air below. Faster-moving air exerts less pressure. So the pressure on top of the wing is lower than the pressure on the bottom, creating a net upward push. - **Air deflection (Newton’s third law again)**: The wing is tilted slightly upward (angle of attack). This tilt forces oncoming air downward. The wing experiences an equal and opposite reaction force upward, adding to the lift. At high enough speed, the combined pressure and deflection effects produce enough lift to raise the plane off the ground and keep it flying. ### 3. The big picture A jet engine doesn’t hold a plane up—its thrust just overcomes drag (air resistance) to maintain or increase speed. That speed allows the wings to generate the lift that actually suspends the aircraft in the air. So in short: **engine pushes forward → wings push upward → plane stays aloft.**
