22:44:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really fair point, and I can absolutely see why you'd think that—if pulling the plug makes it go down, then the engines must be holding it up, right? But there's a crucial distinction: when jet engines fail, the plane does **not** fall out of the sky like a brick. It becomes a glider. Here’s what actually happens: - The moment the engines stop, the plane is still moving forward at high speed (its momentum). - The wings continue to do their job—they generate lift because air is still flowing over them. - Without thrust from the engines, drag (air resistance) gradually slows the plane down. - As it slows, it produces slightly less lift, so it starts to descend. - But that descent trades altitude for speed—gravity pulls the plane downward, which keeps air flowing over the wings, so it maintains controlled flight, just at a gentle downwards angle. This is a glide. Airliners have a significant "glide ratio"—a typical passenger jet can glide forward roughly 15–20 kilometres for every 1 kilometre of altitude it loses. That’s why commercial aircraft at cruising altitude can glide for over 100 km without any engine power. They’re not falling; they’re descending in a controlled, shallow glide. So in the engines-fail scenario: - The plane does eventually go down (no engine to maintain altitude or climb), but **not** straight away like a stone—that proves the wings are still lifting the plane, even without the engines. - What the engines were doing was **overcoming drag** to keep the speed (and thus lift) high enough that the plane could stay at a constant altitude or climb. No engines means it slowly loses that battle to drag, and descends. To go back to the car analogy: If you’re cycling on a flat road and you stop pedalling, you don’t instantly stop dead—you coast for a while before friction brings you to a halt. You wouldn’t say that pedalling was the only thing keeping the bike upright; it was just the thing keeping it *moving forward* against resistance. The plane is similar: engines-on keeps it coasting permanently at altitude; engines-off turns it into a coasting glider slowly losing altitude. So yes, in everyday language, “the engines keep it up” works because they’re what’s needed to sustain level flight. But strictly speaking, the wings are still providing the upward force even when the engines are dead—they just don’t get enough forward speed without thrust to hold the plane at a constant height. The plane falls *gradually*, not instantly, and that’s the big clue that it’s not the engines holding it directly up.
